### What is the Coin Change Minimum Problem?

Given coin denominations and a target amount, return the fewest number of coins needed to make that amount. If the amount cannot be made by any combination of the coins, return -1. Each denomination can be used unlimited times.

At National Bank of India, an ATM must dispense an exact amount using the fewest notes from its loaded cassettes.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class CoinChangeMin {
    public static int minCoins(int[] coins, int amount) {
        int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];
        int sentinel = amount + 1;  // larger than any valid answer
        java.util.Arrays.fill(dp, sentinel);
        dp[0] = 0;

        for (int a = 1; a <= amount; a++) {
            for (int coin : coins) {
                if (coin <= a && dp[a - coin] + 1 < dp[a]) {
                    dp[a] = dp[a - coin] + 1;
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[amount] >= sentinel ? -1 : dp[amount];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] denominations = {1, 5, 10, 25};
        System.out.println("Min coins for 30: " + minCoins(denominations, 30));
        System.out.println("Min coins for 11: " + minCoins(denominations, 11));
    }
}
```

```text
Min coins for 30: 2
Min coins for 11: 2
```

### Why Does Greedy Fail?

The greedy approach (always pick the largest coin that fits) fails for arbitrary denominations. For example, with coins [1, 3, 4] and amount 6: greedy picks 4+1+1=3 coins, but optimal is 3+3=2 coins. DP is required because it considers all possibilities.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class GreedyFails {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] coins = {1, 3, 4};
        int amount = 6;

        // Greedy: pick 4, then 1, then 1 = 3 coins
        System.out.println("Greedy would use 3 coins: 4 + 1 + 1");

        // DP: optimal is 3 + 3 = 2 coins
        System.out.println("DP gives: " + CoinChangeMin.minCoins(coins, amount) + " coins");
    }
}
```

```text
Greedy would use 3 coins: 4 + 1 + 1
DP gives: 2 coins
```

### How Does the DP Table Fill?

We initialize the array with a sentinel value (amount+1) and set dp[0]=0. For each amount from 1 to the target, we try every coin that fits and relax the minimum.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class CoinChangeTrace {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] coins = {1, 3, 4};
        int amount = 6;
        int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(dp, amount + 1);
        dp[0] = 0;

        for (int a = 1; a <= amount; a++) {
            for (int coin : coins) {
                if (coin <= a && dp[a - coin] + 1 < dp[a]) {
                    dp[a] = dp[a - coin] + 1;
                    System.out.println("dp[" + a + "] = " + dp[a] + " (used coin " + coin + ")");
                }
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Answer: " + dp[amount]);
    }
}
```

```text
dp[1] = 1 (used coin 1)
dp[2] = 2 (used coin 1)
dp[3] = 1 (used coin 3)
dp[4] = 1 (used coin 4)
dp[5] = 2 (used coin 1)
dp[6] = 2 (used coin 3)
Answer: 2
```

### How Is This Different from Coin Change Ways?

| Aspect | Coin Change Minimum | Coin Change Ways |
|---|---|---|
| Question | Fewest coins needed | Number of distinct combinations |
| DP value | Minimum count | Number of ways |
| Base case | dp[0] = 0 | dp[0] = 1 |
| Transition | min(dp[a], dp[a-coin]+1) | dp[a] += dp[a-coin] |
| Sentinel | amount+1 for unreachable | 0 (no ways) |
| Loop order | Either order works | Coins outside for combinations |

```java
// Minimum: dp[a] = Math.min(dp[a], dp[a - coin] + 1)
// Ways:    dp[a] += dp[a - coin]
```

### What Are the Edge Cases?

Edge cases include: amount is zero (return 0), single coin equal to amount (return 1), and unreachable amount (return -1).

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class CoinMinEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(CoinChangeMin.minCoins(new int[]{1}, 0));    // 0
        System.out.println(CoinChangeMin.minCoins(new int[]{5}, 5));    // 1
        System.out.println(CoinChangeMin.minCoins(new int[]{2}, 3));    // -1
        System.out.println(CoinChangeMin.minCoins(new int[]{3, 7}, 5)); // -1
    }
}
```

```text
0
1
-1
-1
```

### What About Reconstructing Which Coins Were Used?

To know which coins were used, maintain a parallel array that records which coin was chosen at each amount.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class CoinChangeReconstruct {
    public static List<Integer> reconstruct(int[] coins, int amount) {
        int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];
        int[] chosen = new int[amount + 1];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(dp, amount + 1);
        dp[0] = 0;

        for (int a = 1; a <= amount; a++) {
            for (int coin : coins) {
                if (coin <= a && dp[a - coin] + 1 < dp[a]) {
                    dp[a] = dp[a - coin] + 1;
                    chosen[a] = coin;
                }
            }
        }

        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (dp[amount] >= amount + 1) return result;
        int remaining = amount;
        while (remaining > 0) {
            result.add(chosen[remaining]);
            remaining -= chosen[remaining];
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Coins used: " + reconstruct(new int[]{1, 3, 4}, 6));
    }
}
```

```text
Coins used: [3, 3]
```

### Why Is the Sentinel amount+1 Instead of Integer.MAX_VALUE?

Using `Integer.MAX_VALUE` as the sentinel would cause integer overflow when adding 1 to it. Using `amount + 1` is safe because the maximum number of coins to make any amount is at most `amount` (using all 1-valued coins), so `amount + 1` is guaranteed larger than any valid answer.

```java
// WRONG: Integer.MAX_VALUE + 1 overflows to Integer.MIN_VALUE
int sentinel = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
if (dp[a - coin] + 1 < dp[a])  // BUG: overflow!

// CORRECT: amount + 1 is safe
int sentinel = amount + 1;
if (dp[a - coin] + 1 < dp[a])  // safe comparison
```

### What About Time and Space Complexity?

Time is O(amount * number of coins) because we iterate through all amounts and try all coins at each amount. Space is O(amount) for the DP array.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class CoinMinComplexity {
    // Time: O(amount * coins) -> for each of amount values, try each coin
    // Space: O(amount) -> single DP array
    // This is the unbounded knapsack optimization variant
}
```

### What Are Common Interview Follow-Up Questions?

Interviewers commonly ask:

- Coin Change Ways: count combinations instead of finding the minimum.
- Which coins were used? Maintain a parallel array for reconstruction.
- Limited supply of each coin: bounded knapsack, need high-to-low sweep per coin.
- Can you solve it with BFS? Yes, each state is an amount, edges are coin values, and the shortest path gives the minimum coins.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;

public class CoinChangeBFS {
    public static int minCoinsBFS(int[] coins, int amount) {
        if (amount == 0) return 0;
        boolean[] visited = new boolean[amount + 1];
        Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        queue.add(0);
        visited[0] = true;
        int depth = 0;
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            depth++;
            int size = queue.size();
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                int curr = queue.poll();
                for (int coin : coins) {
                    int next = curr + coin;
                    if (next == amount) return depth;
                    if (next < amount && !visited[next]) {
                        visited[next] = true;
                        queue.add(next);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }
}
```
